WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.181
One clinic has waited six
episodes for its turn.

00:00:03.221 --> 00:00:09.673
Elm: 11 patients, all lower-risk, and not
one readmission. The best record in the cast.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:15.072
So what is Elm's gamma? Ask the
software, and it prints minus 6.25.

00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:20.597
Ask again with its other fitting
routine, and it prints minus 11.52.

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:23.739
Same patients. Same model. Two answers.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:28.633
Which one is right? Neither. And seeing
why shows us what to trust instead.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.421
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:32.461 --> 00:00:34.667
Today: no events, no peak.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:41.122
Here is Elm's own hill: the height,
for each position of its slider.

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:48.361
With the slider at the middle clinic's
level, the height is minus 2.17.

00:00:48.401 --> 00:00:54.461
At minus 3, it is minus 0.53.
At minus 6.25, minus 0.02.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.235
It keeps rising, more and more slowly, toward
a ceiling at zero that it never reaches.

00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:03.526
A ramp, not a hill. There is no top to find.

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:07.709
The meter tells the same story.
Elm observed no readmissions.

00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:12.246
But wherever the slider sits,
the model expects more than none.

00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:17.372
So expected always exceeds observed,
and the meter always says: lower.

00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:24.047
So the slider marches, about one unit a
step, for as long as the fitting runs.

00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:27.573
Stepping together stops
once the dial has settled.

00:01:27.613 --> 00:01:32.266
That takes five steps, and leaves
Elm's slider at minus 6.25.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.776
Taking turns waits for every slider
to settle, and Elm's never will.

00:01:36.816 --> 00:01:41.763
So the software builds a wall,
ten units below the middle clinic.

00:01:41.803 --> 00:01:45.913
Elm's slider runs into it
and stops: minus 11.52.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:49.586
Even the stopping rule matters. Loosen it,

00:01:49.626 --> 00:01:55.450
and stepping together prints minus
5.24. Tighten it: minus 7.25.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.957
Each of these numbers records when an
algorithm stopped. None of them is an estimate.

00:02:00.997 --> 00:02:02.633
And nothing else is harmed.

00:02:02.673 --> 00:02:07.491
The dial and the other four sliders
finish in the same place every time.

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.566
What breaks is anything built on that number.

00:02:11.606 --> 00:02:18.590
Last episode's interval, from minus
19.7 to plus 7.2, was one casualty.

00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:24.176
Here is a worse one. Suppose the reference
is the average gamma, weighted by size.

00:02:24.216 --> 00:02:31.536
That average is minus 1.75 after one
routine, and minus 2.31 after the other.

00:02:31.576 --> 00:02:37.754
Measured against it, Alder's readmissions
come out at 1.87 times expected,

00:02:37.794 --> 00:02:44.278
or 2.76 times. Alder's patients did
not change. Elm's stopping point did.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.251
A clinic with no readmissions has
quietly moved the yardstick for everyone.

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:53.566
What survives is anything built on the count.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:57.074
Take the middle clinic
as the reference instead.

00:02:57.114 --> 00:03:03.236
The median does not care how far out
Elm's slider went: minus 1.52, either way.

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:10.260
At that reference, Elm's 11 patients would
expect 1.97 readmissions. Elm had none.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.874
How surprising is none? Each patient
has an 82% chance of staying out.

00:03:15.914 --> 00:03:18.992
For all eleven, that comes to 11%.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:25.670
Eleven percent is not rare. So Elm is not
flagged. Zero is not automatically excellent.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.068
But thirty such patients with no
readmissions would be another matter:

00:03:30.108 --> 00:03:32.480
about three chances in a thousand.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.366
The count speaks, even when gamma cannot.

00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:39.538
Two footnotes. First, the mirror image:

00:03:39.578 --> 00:03:44.878
a clinic where every patient is
readmitted runs to an upper wall instead.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.941
Second, there was a sixth clinic. Fen,
with 8 patients, never entered the fit,

00:03:49.981 --> 00:03:54.795
because the software screens out any
provider with 10 records or fewer.

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:58.171
Elm, with 11, stayed in by a single patient.

00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:01.263
Let's gather it up.

00:04:01.303 --> 00:04:03.762
No readmissions means a hill with no top,

00:04:03.802 --> 00:04:07.983
and a printed gamma that only
records where the fitting stopped.

00:04:08.023 --> 00:04:12.612
Anything built on that gamma breaks.
Anything built on the count survives.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:16.352
And zero, from a handful of
patients, is weak evidence.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.545
That count, set against what
the reference would expect,

00:04:19.585 --> 00:04:22.839
is the measure this whole
series has been heading for.

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:25.021
Next time: swap the curve.

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:29.272
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
